WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.295
The demonstration uses QPME, the Queueing Petri
Net Modeling Environment. Request paths move

00:00:05.295 --> 00:00:10.416
through modeled activities and transitions.
Queues provide resource contention, while the

00:00:10.416 --> 00:00:14.848
model can represent different request classes
and routing through the system.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.708
An important clarification in the recording is
that each visible green place is not

00:00:19.708 --> 00:00:24.416
necessarily an independent hardware resource.
Several places can reference the same

00:00:24.416 --> 00:00:29.352
underlying queue. In the demonstrated naming
scheme, the prefix identifies the resource

00:00:29.352 --> 00:00:29.692
queue.

00:00:29.692 --> 00:00:34.536
Different request paths can therefore compete
for the same web or authentication resource.

00:00:34.692 --> 00:00:39.861
For each shared queue, inspect scheduling
discipline, number of servers and service-time

00:00:39.861 --> 00:00:45.147
assumptions. For each request class, inspect
routing, visits and synchronization. Counting

00:00:45.147 --> 00:00:50.258
diagram nodes as independent servers would
understate contention and produce misleading

00:00:50.258 --> 00:00:53.606
predictions. Follow one request from entry to
completion.

00:00:53.692 --> 00:00:58.913
For an add-to-cart operation, identify which
modeled activities use the same resource and

00:00:58.913 --> 00:01:04.252
which downstream work the caller waits for.
Write a small activity-to-resource table before

00:01:04.252 --> 00:01:09.181
changing the model. The point is to understand
the semantics behind the diagram, not

00:01:09.181 --> 00:01:10.354
memorize its colors.

00:01:10.354 --> 00:01:15.720
Two places on different request paths both use
the WebUI queue. Do they run on independent

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.027
capacity? No. They share the modeled queue’s
capacity unless the model explicitly assigns

00:01:21.027 --> 00:01:24.843
separate resources. Distinct places can compete
at one resource.

00:01:24.854 --> 00:01:29.955
The demo first adds capacity to a highly
utilized web resource, then explores moving work

00:01:29.955 --> 00:01:34.999
into a separate cart service with asynchronous
interaction. These are different changes:

00:01:34.999 --> 00:01:40.100
adding a server changes resource capacity;
changing the interaction can change the user’s

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.221
waiting path and where work contends.

00:01:42.354 --> 00:01:47.363
Inspect throughput, utilization, population and
residence time as well as end-to-end

00:01:47.363 --> 00:01:52.432
response. A substantial response-time
improvement can occur near saturation even when

00:01:52.432 --> 00:01:57.858
utilization changes more modestly. Conversely, a
quick acknowledgment may leave substantial

00:01:57.858 --> 00:01:59.289
background work running.

00:01:59.354 --> 00:02:04.756
Preserve completion semantics when comparing
synchronous and asynchronous designs. Does the

00:02:04.756 --> 00:02:10.218
response mean the cart update is durable,
accepted for later processing, or fully
completed?

00:02:10.218 --> 00:02:15.442
A shorter response is not equivalent behavior if
the user-visible contract changed. Also

00:02:15.442 --> 00:02:19.539
model retries, ordering, failures and
backpressure where they matter.

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:25.491
Distinguish uncertainty in a simulation estimate
from a latency percentile. A 95% confidence

00:02:25.491 --> 00:02:31.055
interval for a mean is not p95 request latency.
The recording shows simulation output;

00:02:31.055 --> 00:02:36.749
interpret the reported statistic according to
its definition, and avoid transferring its

00:02:36.749 --> 00:02:39.143
example numbers to a real deployment.

00:02:39.143 --> 00:02:44.765
Moving work off the response path lowers
acknowledgment time. Has the total work
necessarily

00:02:44.765 --> 00:02:50.327
decreased? No. The work may continue elsewhere,
with similar utilization or new contention.

00:02:50.327 --> 00:02:55.888
Compare the same completion semantics and
inspect downstream capacity and failure
behavior.

00:02:55.888 --> 00:03:02.735
The interactive lab is a deliberately smaller
model than the lecture’s QPN demonstration. It

00:03:02.735 --> 00:03:09.060
assumes one M/M/1 queue: Poisson arrivals,
independent exponential service times, one

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:15.832
server, an unbounded queue and steady state.
With service demand S seconds and arrival rate

00:03:15.832 --> 00:03:22.232
λ per second, utilization is ρ = λS and mean
response is R = S/(1−ρ), only when ρ < 1.

00:03:22.388 --> 00:03:31.478
New worked example: λ = 75 requests/second and S
= 8 ms gives ρ = 0.60 and R = 20 ms.

00:03:31.478 --> 00:03:41.102
Increasing S to 10 ms gives ρ = 0.75 and R = 40
ms. A 25% increase in local demand doubles

00:03:41.102 --> 00:03:43.883
the model’s mean response.

00:03:43.888 --> 00:03:48.798
At λS ≥ 1 the model has no finite steady-state
mean; reporting a negative response from the

00:03:48.798 --> 00:03:50.200
formula would be an error.

00:03:50.388 --> 00:03:55.837
Use this calculation to explain amplification,
not to claim every request follows an

00:03:55.837 --> 00:04:01.805
exponential distribution. A real model may need
multiple resources, several visits, parallel

00:04:01.805 --> 00:04:07.514
branches, finite queues and distinct workload
classes. Revalidate after an architectural

00:04:07.514 --> 00:04:13.157
change. A reviewable analysis records the
baseline measurements, local change, mapping,

00:04:13.157 --> 00:04:17.114
model assumptions, workload, prediction and
validation error.

00:04:17.114 --> 00:04:22.380
Keep calibration evidence separate from
validation evidence. Recommend a measurement or

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:27.827
experiment that would most reduce uncertainty in
the decision. Why is R = S/(1−λS) invalid

00:04:27.827 --> 00:04:33.274
as a finite steady-state estimate when λS
reaches one? Arrivals consume all or more of the

00:04:33.274 --> 00:04:34.303
service capacity.

00:04:34.303 --> 00:04:39.126
The assumed queue has no stable finite mean
response; the algebra is not a valid

00:04:39.126 --> 00:04:41.779
continuation beyond the stability condition.
